---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const site = Astro.site?.href ?? 'https://88cutouts.com/';

const jsonld = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: 'About 88cutouts',
    url: new URL('/about/', site).href,
    isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: '88cutouts', url: site },
    mainEntity: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: '88cutouts',
      url: site,
      email: 'hello@88cutouts.com',
      founder: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Sarath' },
      description:
        'A small, hand-kept library of free transparent PNG stickers, frames and cutouts for Instagram Stories.',
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About 88cutouts — free stickers for Instagram Stories"
  description="Who makes 88cutouts, how the stickers are produced, what the licence covers, and how to get in touch. A small, free library of transparent PNG cutouts."
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>About 88cutouts</h1>
      <p>
        88cutouts is a small, hand-kept library of transparent PNG stickers, frames and
        letter cutouts made for one thing: decorating Instagram Stories. Some are drawn, some
        photographed and some rendered, and every one is cut out by hand and published as a
        clean alpha-transparent PNG so it drops onto a Story without a white box around it.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is run by one person. That is the reason the library is a few hundred pieces
        rather than a few hundred thousand, and it is also the reason each one actually
        works when you paste it.
      </p>

      <h2>How the cutouts are made</h2>
      <p>
        Each piece starts as artwork or a photograph, and then goes through the same four
        steps before it is published:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cut out by hand.</strong> Not auto-traced. Automatic background removal
          leaves ragged edges and eats thin details like ribbon ends and whiskers, which is
          why so many free stickers look approximately right and never quite paste cleanly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Edge decontamination.</strong> When an image is cut from a white
          background, the semi-transparent pixels at the boundary keep a little of that
          white in them. On a dark photo that shows up as a faint grey halo. Removing it is
          the step most tutorials skip, and it is the difference between a sticker that
          sits on a photo and one that floats above it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Trimmed to the artwork</strong> — across most of the library. Some newer
          uploads still sit inside a transparent margin, which makes them paste in smaller
          than the box around them. Where that is the case, the sticker's own page says so
          and gives the artwork's real dimensions next to the file's.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Normalised to a 900 pixel maximum edge</strong> and exported as PNG-32.
          Large enough to fill a good part of a 1080 × 1920 Story without softening, small
          enough that Copy is instant on a phone connection.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you want to do the same thing yourself, the whole process is written up in
        <a href="/blog/how-to-make-transparent-png-stickers/">making transparent PNG stickers</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How to use them</h2>
      <p>
        There is nothing to sign up for. Tap <strong>Copy</strong> on any sticker and it
        goes to your phone's clipboard as an image — then long-press inside the Instagram
        Story editor and paste. If your browser doesn't support copying images,
        <strong>Download</strong> always works as a fallback. One thing worth knowing:
        Instagram <em>feed</em> posts flatten transparency to grey. Stories, DMs and most
        messengers keep it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every sticker page also has an opacity control. It changes the file, not just the
        preview, so you can copy the same cutout solid for a plain background or faded down
        to 20% to sit over a photo like a watermark.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="/ransom-note/">cutout text maker</a> works the same way — tap a phrase
        or type your own, and get a paper-letter collage back as a transparent PNG. The
        <a href="/dp/">DP gallery</a> is the other section: ready-made square profile
        pictures composed for the circular crop that WhatsApp and Instagram apply.
      </p>

      <h2>Licence — what you can and can't do</h2>
      <p>
        Everything here is <strong>free for personal use</strong>. In practice that means:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Yes:</strong> your Instagram Stories, posts and highlights, your group
          chats, your notes app, your phone wallpaper, a birthday card for a friend, a
          school project.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No:</strong> reselling the files, bundling them into a paid sticker pack,
          redistributing the library as your own, or uploading them to a stock site.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        No attribution is required and there is no watermark on anything. If you want to use
        something commercially, ask — the answer is often yes, it just needs to be asked.
        There is more on how this fits with copyright generally in
        <a href="/blog/free-to-use-images-copyright-explained/">free-to-use images and copyright</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How the site is kept</h2>
      <p>
        New cutouts go up a few times a week, in small batches rather than bulk imports.
        Anything that turns out not to paste cleanly gets fixed or removed. The blog posts
        are written to be read once and remembered, not to rank for a keyword — if a guide
        here is longer than it needs to be, that is a mistake rather than a strategy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing you do on this site is uploaded anywhere. Copying a sticker, setting an
        opacity and generating cutout text all happen inside your own browser. The details
        of what is and is not collected are on the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Made by Sarath. Requests for a sticker that does not exist yet, corrections,
        licensing questions and takedown requests all go to the same place:
        <a href="mailto:hello@88cutouts.com">hello@88cutouts.com</a>. If you spot an asset
        that should not be here, say so and it comes down while I look into it.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
